ion-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/461a35869623099aa0b6466633af6cb58920f657ab879d9cb7ccae0a05481e75.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/257654dafc0b541dfdd00250997596cbb35da44d4bf5b201911661459fac3617.png) is homogeneous of degree ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/56de185c0e7e838b89aec2ec1cee1ebc90029112047f9cebc42de13a1bcf3f95.png) if it satisfies the property\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/a1ef987a703c142bd1aeb52c5a46782e54601ecf0f3176f3ba77953b5ad3e9b1.png)\n\nSo first, we compute ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/982da4ce20585fff559fe51384832ff7ba9ab10a7ad7ddc31831c78a3a0972e5.png) and then we simplify by writing it as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/8808adb23bc56b3bd8dcb3427d3db0c8043fb26fa6cdcf5cc2cf0ca0d0615c94.png) for some ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/07e057b287d9c0cb5eec4b825014b42dfb37092d6592d225299b1096ebe08bb5.png):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/acead36aa36a5f642d2b5d408f390634be3d01274abfb6a4196d8d332a0f82d5.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/b7a2044031e99d2071771a1c3d1f71d6a4fd07a57b283c081d40939525b73254.png)\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/47070dbaf25d08b50c3f42c5f4ef9ce7d02b4e541112fa3049126f2d5cdcffed.png) is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/bf820cdf3770e08974a14218498296ee4390accf8a18601af55422206ce41385.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "f0a5e360-e989-5252-b4dc-d53336e982fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-2298" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x,y,z) = x^3+2y^3+z^3$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =(tx)^{3}+2(ty)^{3}+(tz)^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}{x}^{3}+2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9a070d56-1aac-5f5d-885c-790d75571c63" :question/math-academy-id "e-2299" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/id #uuid "5def8755-3a1d-5f9c-a9f1-ac51d4ed5b13" :question/math-academy-id "e-2300" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n\n- $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n\n- $h(x,y) = 6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2,$ while the other terms have degree $1.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6,$ and all of its terms are of degree $6.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0,$ and all of its terms are of degree $0.$ (There is only one term, and it has degree $0.$)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d18b709-9455-5bc9-86ec-d0ac91ef6080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146402} {:answer/id #uuid "98e38005-12ac-5cfd-aa57-c975399a303d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146403} {:answer/id #uuid "0ecee130-60b9-5650-9efa-44ff59bffc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146404} {:answer/id #uuid "1269ab84-21ee-5242-ae2e-18c85fbd16ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146405} {:answer/id #uuid "7516b052-0c97-55f7-aaa1-94d2518d1bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d18b709-9455-5bc9-86ec-d0ac91ef6080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146402} :answer-field/id #uuid "49709f74-e3d8-5776-8f6b-8eabb11ef0b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d29f021-f031-5e6d-9100-cd4401012cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64175" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}+xy-4{z}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =3(tx)^{2}+(tx)(ty)-4(tz)^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy-4{t}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+xy-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{2}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33bae967-7c4a-5964-941c-e4c532295244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146408} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9011ef-59b4-5d3b-85aa-f8d7ca4d9235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146409} {:answer/id #uuid "4c418789-6b19-55fc-8c11-a847b2996863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146410} {:answer/id #uuid "61829241-f402-5ad3-a42b-7f7ceaebeb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146411} {:answer/id #uuid "66de759e-4b59-50dd-92cb-5e3223106a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66de759e-4b59-50dd-92cb-5e3223106a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146412} :answer-field/id #uuid "9314db51-8457-5087-8ce8-01990307eb9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5a7e39d-2b55-5b19-ad1b-291a9a1c5223" :question/math-academy-id "q-64176" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x)=2x+1$\n- $g(x,y)={x}^{3}+3xy$\n- $h(x,y)={x}^{5}-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $1.$ However, it also contains a constant term. Therefore not all of its terms are of degree $1.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $3.$ However, it also contains a term ($3xy$) that is of degree $2.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $3.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $5.$ It is a polynomial of degree $5,$ and all of its terms are of degree $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec728116-cf65-5473-b63d-21c3e13afe63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322051} {:answer/id #uuid "24dac729-c4a2-5b85-b9fa-806eb0bcd744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322052} {:answer/id #uuid "f740ae6a-ed59-5897-bbf6-f3c25555cb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322053} {:answer/id #uuid "3551b1b7-6fd6-599a-a7a7-4a245ee8ca5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322054} {:answer/id #uuid "f41f2221-c162-5699-9560-0cccb211c546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f41f2221-c162-5699-9560-0cccb211c546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d343bb85-be51-5ab3-9b47-5fae731c4cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ab09b8d-c62f-59d4-a051-b0634322aef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64179" :question/problem "Is the function $g(x,y)=2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we try to simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =2(tx)^{3}+3(ty)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}{x}^{3}+3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}g(x,y)={t}^{n}(2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2})\n$$\n\nand so $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd60a862-75cb-5583-ba7e-d15ca3d53395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322058} {:answer/id #uuid "80df5bf2-82a3-5520-b547-b636048cca37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} {:answer/id #uuid "ae28ebe7-48ee-5c37-ad70-347e63720d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322060} {:answer/id #uuid "8f77516d-4cde-561b-9cc5-d71367490fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322061} {:answer/id #uuid "9c761827-1e1e-5344-8247-75bdf7dbeda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80df5bf2-82a3-5520-b547-b636048cca37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4505c20-b837-51a5-b06d-f2bf8603d0f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1f59ac7-fe59-5414-84e5-39e2cb13a6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64198" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(y)=5{y}^{2}$\n- $g(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{4}$\n- $h(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-xyz$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$ It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant term.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $5.$ However, it also contains a term ($2{x}^{3}y$) that is of degree $4.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $5.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebdff1ee-3c16-5775-8f0d-d260e1541ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146450} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa1129e-7d7d-57a4-b8f2-c82994e86bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146451} {:answer/id #uuid "145c005e-3c5b-5362-8414-d4193f2a88bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146452} {:answer/id #uuid "231be162-2cdf-55d6-9520-38dc12d4a66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146453} {:answer/id #uuid "4753d16e-f27a-50a6-9de8-3f03cd768eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4753d16e-f27a-50a6-9de8-3f03cd768eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146454} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ee7000-b424-5c53-9f1a-00c1398e11a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "deb5eb69-50f7-5bca-9d1d-4019af78a79b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64199" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-2=0.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)-(tx)^{2}}{3(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}xy-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(xy-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ef4c83-bb89-5f50-9623-e35ef6c81ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322065} {:answer/id #uuid "890a71b1-a389-5054-903c-8cd38b043e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322066} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bc7514-f20f-5675-bc4a-5a417d736d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322067} {:answer/id #uuid "c50aedc9-8afa-5edf-b10b-190e9366624a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322068} {:answer/id #uuid "0f492ce2-7d59-504d-98f8-6a050c730d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5bc7514-f20f-5675-bc4a-5a417d736d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322067} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dfa529-3cec-5997-ba2d-58f2ae12b76c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ac6de04-c052-57c1-969b-cc05e34d199a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64200" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3.$\n\nTherefore, the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-3=1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}+4(ty)^{4}}{2(ty)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}+4{t}^{4}{y}^{4}}{2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4})}{{t}^{3}(2{y}^{3})} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =th(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=th(x,y)={t}^{1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f0fcf48-5fec-5e42-b451-6fbd966fa808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322072} {:answer/id #uuid "ab59215e-1a30-5f99-a031-ac19926709ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322073} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb1eb35-5d95-5d73-97a8-fc216d19c8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322074} {:answer/id #uuid "b26f55e1-684c-57b7-a33e-8038bf020c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322075} {:answer/id #uuid "27d2992d-f8eb-5cca-9bf7-df6878c0e595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27d2992d-f8eb-5cca-9bf7-df6878c0e595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322076} :answer-field/id #uuid "b51e107b-3623-5a0e-bd0c-f28a2f6cd016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ef1e233-ce29-56eb-9a97-4a5290698d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64201" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-4=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)^{2}+(tx)(ty)}{2(tx)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy}{2{t}^{4}{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(5{y}^{2}+xy)}{{t}^{4}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae178ad1-b94f-5ffc-90a2-a3384524cfa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322079} {:answer/id #uuid "7879a9be-6f89-5f1b-a6e8-335e5bc09b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322080} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdb67e6-ad34-5b59-b888-d004e24d62ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} {:answer/id #uuid "17954489-6666-5652-9291-67582548e6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322082} {:answer/id #uuid "433d2eef-57f7-5a4f-acec-d5fafbd80e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfdb67e6-ad34-5b59-b888-d004e24d62ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d851aee-96bf-5565-a690-707722840eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a46a6a02-6826-5c1a-9f58-69b0f1d3511a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64202" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{3tx-ty}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{ty}{t(3x-y)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{3x-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=1\\cdot g(x,y)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eaa7052-aa9e-5346-809c-c002c00af207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146456} {:answer/id #uuid "d609b146-13c2-5255-bf32-74457f93e32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146457} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7ea8c4-3c52-5c6c-b486-14507817cb9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146458} {:answer/id #uuid "317d41e4-a299-5f9a-9470-540022d19b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146459} {:answer/id #uuid "7601096c-9c17-57dd-ba1f-00e3d856b44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d609b146-13c2-5255-bf32-74457f93e32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146457} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d69b07-95e3-5edc-9b78-45c776eda9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8184c9d-59e3-5623-bc59-3b1dfdec057b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64204" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)=\\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)}{2(tx)^{2}+8(tx)(ty)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5ty}{2{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+8{t}^{2}xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5ty}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+8xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{t(2{x}^{2}+8xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot h(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot h(x,y)={t}^{-1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)$ is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf79f29-4f23-5a4b-8f84-40855e80f8c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322086} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f49e8b-71bf-57f9-a0f2-8ce891d992f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322087} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc49884-faf8-514b-a926-c2b9a302e846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322088} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3f8410-10db-5d74-a1e9-63e4a44f3fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322089} {:answer/id #uuid "61a13a79-d306-562a-92e7-a321f454fba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc49884-faf8-514b-a926-c2b9a302e846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322088} :answer-field/id #uuid "30d6d841-7e53-5014-9e4a-8351c523a36e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26800737-1d20-5fc8-93e8-d8bcbcdef142" :question/math-academy-id "q-79577" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)(ty)(tz)-(ty)^{3}+(ty)^{2}(tz) \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}xyz-{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{y}^{2}z \\\\  & ={t}^{3}(2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f671610-4a25-5741-9312-4b208402a02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322093} {:answer/id #uuid "82836f60-ac9e-592d-bdfd-d3a976b92dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322094} {:answer/id #uuid "7154723b-d115-5d6d-97fe-4f88b36d107c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322095} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8b9ceb-4d97-5c2e-a469-72cfe2b322c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322096} {:answer/id #uuid "352460e9-f907-5659-997b-eb743962e763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82836f60-ac9e-592d-bdfd-d3a976b92dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322094} :answer-field/id #uuid "58899065-cf86-5331-a128-02b0f5cc0fe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c5da278-e488-5961-b0d5-3c97c074b513" :question/math-academy-id "q-79578" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x)=3xy$\n- $g(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}y$\n- $h(x,y)={x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$  It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant terms.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $3.$ However, it also contains a term $({y}^{2})$ that is of degree $2.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $3.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a914889-f972-56f6-93d7-8ec526ca79e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322100} {:answer/id #uuid "01685af9-2e2d-5100-a873-2d735afaeeb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322101} {:answer/id #uuid "823394d3-aa4b-55d5-91a8-670b37a3d0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322102} {:answer/id #uuid "26872bf7-671e-5a27-9cc1-29c0815d4f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322103} {:answer/id #uuid "a21f4c58-6715-5c30-8c2b-5827a802d2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26872bf7-671e-5a27-9cc1-29c0815d4f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322103} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9104691-ae71-5384-b59b-3636f8409678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43b3d904-b08a-5c5e-b242-5fe6d02d0e66" :question/math-academy-id "q-79581" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}-(tx)^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}-{t}^{3}{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}-{x}^{3})}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y)={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b8c303-b28a-544d-8531-74de5c8ffc42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322107} {:answer/id #uuid "85ec20b2-6ad2-5c22-b151-579fa41a5bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322108} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae25217-a2a8-52e7-891f-64221d356281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322109} {:answer/id #uuid "69979a22-ac07-5e00-9b34-280361fd14fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322110} {:answer/id #uuid "2b18b304-230c-517c-9f7a-46de58dcbf63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b18b304-230c-517c-9f7a-46de58dcbf63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322111} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb13a5f-fd7f-558d-a248-60b080ad5b40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1622bb44-831b-5f90-acde-f314e5aa270b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90572" :question/problem "Is the function $g(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}+5{z}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}g(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)^{2}+(tx)(ty)^{2}+5(tz)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{y}^{2}+5{t}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}g(x,y,z)={t}^{n}(2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}+5{z}^{2})\n$$\n\nand so $g(x,y,z)$ is not homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218420a5-304b-59f7-a93b-75f9c3d102ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322114} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1b33f5-0d94-5c82-8322-944c2fd933b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322115} {:answer/id #uuid "7253fa58-4d69-55d7-b438-21371c8a31fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322116} {:answer/id #uuid "fcef85b6-28a7-597c-b567-e65570312614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322117} {:answer/id #uuid "15818b96-bf95-5c54-8914-263c09b9e6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15818b96-bf95-5c54-8914-263c09b9e6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c74cb26-0167-5164-b3ca-4b9a7fbdf56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e36fadc1-ee32-54a1-9136-21e4fbf1caad" :question/math-academy-id "q-90573" :question/problem "Is the function $h(x,y,z)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}-8{z}^{3}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}h(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty,tz) & =(tx)^{3}(ty)+(tx)(ty)^{3}-8(tz)^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}{x}^{3}y+{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}-8{t}^{3}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}h(x,y,z)={t}^{n}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}-8{z}^{3})\n$$\n\nand so $h(x,y,z)$ is not homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5462d0d5-f197-58c6-ad6f-c03cfe8d6f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322121} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc8cdc-8955-50a3-bfdb-ac4f1c238b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322122} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9fdb9c-fd23-518a-a6ea-aa68bfdae880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322123} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb695a8-8d9a-5a05-a0bd-2526893f9ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322124} {:answer/id #uuid "fffa18b7-4eeb-5869-95ec-efe1e68446fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5462d0d5-f197-58c6-ad6f-c03cfe8d6f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322121} :answer-field/id #uuid "71826cb2-d3fc-5862-a2c1-10bd50eba439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "056f268e-6f23-5cca-ba50-28679fda2fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-90580" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x)=7{x}^{7}$\n- $g(x,y)=x+y$\n- $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z-2xyz+x{z}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $7.$  It is a polynomial of degree $7,$ with no terms of degree other than $7.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$ It is a polynomial of degree $1,$ and all of its terms are of degree $1.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is  homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1828dd33-b40a-5c88-a28b-fa036e1855b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322128} {:answer/id #uuid "55fb6df6-0f8d-546b-831d-4aea587f33c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322129} {:answer/id #uuid "cb33fa20-a90e-5e8d-a85b-c3373dabae8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322130} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9167b0-29e3-5817-87c8-fce4fdfa391d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322131} {:answer/id #uuid "8581da7a-a4b3-5ebd-9040-6415f148e75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1828dd33-b40a-5c88-a28b-fa036e1855b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322128} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbeb3d0a-d63d-5e7f-948f-aeb78827f419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6efb05be-f935-56f9-98cd-ac76c6dde006" :question/math-academy-id "q-90582" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(y)={y}^{2}+y$\n- $g(x,y)={x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}$\n- $h(x,y,z)=xyz$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$  is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2.$ However, it also contains a term ($y$) that is of degree $1.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $2.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4.$ It is a polynomial of degree $4,$ and all of its terms are of degree $4.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ with no terms of degree other than $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "953c2e68-8113-5a93-9006-d80e85e73567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322135} {:answer/id #uuid "b161eb63-e9cb-5ead-ae51-3c26a227a501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322136} {:answer/id #uuid "88e193d0-8014-5880-bf08-dfcc62ef0807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322137} {:answer/id #uuid "08efc616-98d7-5c4f-b6d3-12a134d11a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322138} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5c06d9-e4ef-50aa-9f5b-99f04937f098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b161eb63-e9cb-5ead-ae51-3c26a227a501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322136} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aacdfd2-6bef-52b8-939d-7623e47756c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "431e940d-dc2f-5be2-ab99-48a93496e073" :question/math-academy-id "q-90588" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-2=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{4}}{2(tx)(ty)-6(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}{x}^{4}}{2{t}^{2}xy-6{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}({x}^{4})}{{t}^{2}(2xy-6{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{2}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d047bd4-2804-5336-aaba-c10002d15c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322142} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f2413-dbc0-577d-a215-c975da5ac20f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322143} {:answer/id #uuid "4216c417-13f1-53ba-a647-1ba8ed35faa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322144} {:answer/id #uuid "26220314-b114-5eca-b0c5-f8051d2a8451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322145} {:answer/id #uuid "69eb8e09-6283-5c5c-9550-fd5b7919230c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d047bd4-2804-5336-aaba-c10002d15c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322142} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a3ff59-8cc6-5027-966f-2e310f50916f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28bf47fa-7b88-58b0-b97d-7e859e594577" :question/math-academy-id "q-90589" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-1=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}}{4(ty)-(tx)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}}{4ty-tx} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3})}{t(4y-x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{3}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096cb871-9cd2-5381-b1d6-302d1d8a49b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322149} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d8e547-67e0-5341-bcc3-0b33d601d366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322150} {:answer/id #uuid "01f568b5-4c09-551f-8de5-471218c5e535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322151} {:answer/id #uuid "5e93f0a0-4ccf-5a85-a46d-63601d7f7f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322152} {:answer/id #uuid "55dbf86a-4da3-5e8c-8f2d-027af549b1b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e93f0a0-4ccf-5a85-a46d-63601d7f7f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26b0697-9c74-532b-9d37-bb7892b296f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0b33e13-a7b4-5f66-b92e-9cd183c50e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-90594" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{tx}{2ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2271484-8110-5406-8f65-2e5682e1dc94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322156} {:answer/id #uuid "e072fdd9-9cb1-5911-a1f1-01e5e15a6442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322157} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a01dec-7eb3-5d97-ad26-0a157af1d246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322158} {:answer/id #uuid "e95298d0-30ed-56a2-bbc1-3a057cf0157e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322159} {:answer/id #uuid "8a744c81-313c-5e0d-9f0f-024de1e4ad7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e072fdd9-9cb1-5911-a1f1-01e5e15a6442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322157} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a01898-c253-5883-a712-a2c5639abd26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01a90fbd-9569-5668-b55c-c57b9ff2d6b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90596" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{4(tx)^{2}-(ty)^{2}}{(tx)}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =tg(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=tg(x,y)={t}^{1}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $1.$"}]}
